2
00:00:00.632 --> 00:00:01.263
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:01.263 --> 00:00:03.579
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>And listen to it and read whatever we write about it.

4
00:00:03.579 --> 00:00:04.000
<v MYRTLE MARIE ROSAMORE>Wow.

5
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:09.547
<v ARSENEAULT>And if you do say anything on the interview that you don’t want in the record, we can just erase that.

6
00:00:09.547 --> 00:00:09.811
<v ROSAMORE>Okay.

7
00:00:09.811 --> 00:00:11.132
<v ARSENEAULT>So that’s up to you.

8
00:00:11.132 --> 00:00:15.623
Like whatever . . . If you end up saying, “Oh I don’t want that to go on the record.”

9
00:00:15.623 --> 00:00:15.887
<v ROSAMORE>Right.

10
00:00:15.887 --> 00:00:18.000
<v ARSENEAULT>We can just kind of write out . . .

11
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:18.400
<v ROSAMORE>Okay.

12
00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:28.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And then saying that any pictures we take we can put on the Facebook account, and use that for the record as well.

13
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:28.344
<v ROSAMORE>Okay.

14
00:00:28.344 --> 00:00:28.688
<v ARSENEAULT>So . . .

15
00:00:28.688 --> 00:00:30.750
<v ROSAMORE>I mean I have no . . . I’m . . .

16
00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:31.094
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

17
00:00:31.094 --> 00:00:31.438
Great.

18
00:00:31.438 --> 00:00:33.500
You can just sign right there.

19
00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:39.000
<v ROSAMORE>Because I basically . . . The ones that I brought, I don’t really care if it’s public.

20
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:39.375
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

21
00:00:39.375 --> 00:00:41.250
<v ROSAMORE>You know what I’m saying?

22
00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:41.625
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

23
00:00:41.625 --> 00:00:43.875
I’m going to go ahead . . . Great!

24
00:00:43.875 --> 00:00:44.250
Alright.

25
00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:48.750
I will just get my questions from you . . . I think we’re good.

26
00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:52.125
And do we need to ask for anything else?

27
00:00:52.125 --> 00:00:53.625
Paperwork’s signed . . . We’re good.

28
00:00:53.625 --> 00:00:54.750
Are we on?

29
00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:55.125
<v CHARLOTTE WILLCOX>Yeah.

30
00:00:55.125 --> 00:00:55.500
<v ARSENEAULT>Alright.

31
00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:55.875
Great.

32
00:00:55.875 --> 00:00:57.000
<v ROSAMORE>Oh, wow.

33
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.615
<v ARSENEAULT>So I’m going to start us off with a little introduction, and then I’m just going to ask you a couple of basic questions.

34
00:01:01.615 --> 00:01:02.000
<v ROSAMORE>Okay.

35
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:03.333
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s how it works.

36
00:01:03.333 --> 00:01:03.667
Okay.

37
00:01:03.667 --> 00:01:11.667
Today is January twenty-third, 2015 and I’m Chelsea Arseneault representing the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

38
00:01:11.667 --> 00:01:18.000
We’re working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum and Bill Shearman to document the history of Mossville, Louisiana.

39
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:25.333
And to kick that off, we are holding Mossville oral history day here at the Rigmaiden Recreation Center on Old Spanish Trail.

40
00:01:25.333 --> 00:01:29.667
I’m here today with Ms. Myrtle Rosamore, is that how you say it?

41
00:01:29.667 --> 00:01:30.000
<v ROSAMORE>Yes.

42
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:30.333
<v ARSENEAULT>Rosamore.

43
00:01:30.333 --> 00:01:31.000
<v ROSAMORE>[Agrees].

44
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:36.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Thank you so much for coming and talking with us and agreeing to be interviewed.

45
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.000
This is going to be a short interview of about fifteen minutes,

46
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:47.333
and we’ll likely be in touch with you later to set up a much longer interview if you’re interested in continuing on.

47
00:01:47.333 --> 00:01:47.667
<v ROSAMORE>Okay.

48
00:01:47.667 --> 00:01:48.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Great.

49
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:48.667
<v ROSAMORE>Sounds good.

50
00:01:48.667 --> 00:01:49.333
<v ARSENEAULT>Alright great.

51
00:01:49.333 --> 00:01:53.333
Just for the record can you please just state your full name?

52
00:01:53.333 --> 00:01:54.333
<v ROSAMORE>Myrtle Marie Rosamore.

53
00:01:54.333 --> 00:01:57.667
<v ARSENEAULT>And where were you born, and what was the year?

54
00:01:57.667 --> 00:01:58.000
<v ROSAMORE>Okay.

55
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:03.333
I was born in Lake Charles at the Moss Regional Hospital, and that was in 1960.

56
00:02:03.333 --> 00:02:03.667
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

57
00:02:03.667 --> 00:02:06.000
And what are your parents’ names?

58
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.250
<v ROSAMORE>Elmer and Georgia Braxton.

59
00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:12.500
<v ARSENEAULT>And your grandparents’ names?

60
00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:15.750
<v ROSAMORE>[Aretha?] and Tom Rigmaiden.

61
00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:19.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, the center . . .

62
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:23.119
<v ROSAMORE>No, no. No, that’s my great grand . . . I’m sorry.

63
00:02:23.119 --> 00:02:26.322
Aretha and Lee Braxton was my grandparents.

64
00:02:26.322 --> 00:02:28.153
<v ARSENEAULT>And your great grandparents?

65
00:02:28.153 --> 00:02:29.525
Their last names . . .?

66
00:02:29.525 --> 00:02:35.017
<v ROSAMORE>Tom Rigmaiden and Melissa Perkins, but she was a Rigmaiden by marriage.

67
00:02:35.017 --> 00:02:37.763
But that was her maiden name.

68
00:02:37.763 --> 00:02:43.254
<v ARSENEAULT>So you are in the Rigmaiden Center today, so that’s kind of . . .

69
00:02:43.254 --> 00:02:44.627
<v ROSAMORE>Pretty much . . . Family.

70
00:02:44.627 --> 00:02:45.085
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

71
00:02:45.085 --> 00:02:46.000
<v ROSAMORE>[Agrees].

72
00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:46.376
<v ARSENEAULT>Great.

73
00:02:46.376 --> 00:02:47.127
That’s awesome.

74
00:02:47.127 --> 00:02:49.755
What is your earliest memory of Mossville?

75
00:02:49.755 --> 00:02:51.633
<v ROSAMORE>When I was about . . . seven.

76
00:02:51.633 --> 00:02:58.767
That’s about when I really realized . . . But I’ve always had family around, like my dad’s sisters and all them.

77
00:02:58.767 --> 00:03:02.898
So the earliest I can remember was when I was seven.

78
00:03:02.898 --> 00:03:08.906
And what made me remember that . . . because one of my cousins had came to the house.

79
00:03:08.906 --> 00:03:17.167
And he had got stung by wasps and he was allergic to them and both of his eyes were real real swollen.

80
00:03:17.167 --> 00:03:20.922
And that’s when I . . . That’s what I relate Mossville to.

81
00:03:20.922 --> 00:03:23.176
When I was younger, is that.

82
00:03:23.176 --> 00:03:28.057
And plus my dad used to have chickens and ducks and all that,

83
00:03:28.057 --> 00:03:33.314
so he would take me with him to feed those and stuff like that.

84
00:03:33.314 --> 00:03:37.069
So I mean it wasn’t a whole lot at seven.

85
00:03:37.069 --> 00:03:40.824
Then when I got older I really remembered a lot.

86
00:03:40.824 --> 00:03:43.078
We used to camp a lot.

87
00:03:43.078 --> 00:03:52.090
Used to take us camping and stuff like fishing and I’ve been doing that all my life now from just what he taught me.

88
00:03:52.090 --> 00:03:56.971
And well, the family all did hunting, and fishing, and stuff like that.

89
00:03:56.971 --> 00:04:04.106
So it’s pretty much I just fell in with everybody because I used to hunt with my daddy, too.

90
00:04:04.106 --> 00:04:08.612
So . . . pretty much . . . I mean that’s the earliest that I can remember,

91
00:04:08.612 --> 00:04:11.616
but then the latest is until I’m . . . now.

92
00:04:11.616 --> 00:04:11.992
Yeah.

93
00:04:11.992 --> 00:04:12.743
Pretty much.

94
00:04:12.743 --> 00:04:18.000
Fifty-three, so I’m fifty-four, but when I left here I was fifty-three, so.

95
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.903
All my life that’s all I knew was Mossville.

96
00:04:20.903 --> 00:04:22.516
My kids grew up here.

97
00:04:22.516 --> 00:04:28.645
I raised them here and I raised them with the knowledge that I know and I grew up with,

98
00:04:28.645 --> 00:04:32.516
so it’s kind of like . . . But it has changed through the years.

99
00:04:32.516 --> 00:04:37.355
I mean it used to be we wouldn’t see a car but every three hours.

100
00:04:37.355 --> 00:04:41.226
And then it came from that to it’s like a busy street.

101
00:04:41.226 --> 00:04:44.129
And I remember seeing when the roads were gravel.

102
00:04:44.129 --> 00:04:51.226
Because we used to . . . I mean all the roads in Mossville were gravel, and then all of the sudden they became blacktop.

103
00:04:51.226 --> 00:04:54.129
So that let us know that times was changing.

104
00:04:54.129 --> 00:04:55.742
You know what I’m saying?

105
00:04:55.742 --> 00:04:57.032
Then the refineries came.

106
00:04:57.032 --> 00:04:59.935
Well, I don’t remember them when I was little

107
00:04:59.935 --> 00:05:07.355
<c.p>because I was too young to know, but then when I got older and started having children, then I knew they were there.

108
00:05:07.355 --> 00:05:14.452
But it . . . I mean we had to live where we had to live because that’s where we was brought up and raised.

109
00:05:14.452 --> 00:05:14.774
<v ARSENEAULT>Right.

110
00:05:14.774 --> 00:05:17.355
<v ROSAMORE>So pretty much that was about it, really.

111
00:05:17.355 --> 00:05:18.000
That.

112
00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:20.722
<v ARSENEAULT>You mentioned feeding ducks with your father.

113
00:05:20.722 --> 00:05:21.111
<v ROSAMORE>[Agrees].

114
00:05:21.111 --> 00:05:23.444
<v ARSENEAULT>Did your family have a garden?

115
00:05:23.444 --> 00:05:28.111
<v ROSAMORE>Well my . . . See my mom’s mom, she used to live with us.

116
00:05:28.111 --> 00:05:32.778
But I was older then, and she used to have a garden.

117
00:05:32.778 --> 00:05:35.889
She used to do all kind of vegetables.

118
00:05:35.889 --> 00:05:40.167
And her thing was okras, and peppers, and stuff like that.

119
00:05:40.167 --> 00:05:49.111
But that’s the only garden I remember because my dad did the ducks, the chickens, the gathering up the eggs, and all that.

120
00:05:49.111 --> 00:05:54.167
But the only garden I remember is my grandma on my momma’s side.

121
00:05:54.167 --> 00:06:00.000
But she lived here with us too, so . . . Until she passed on pretty much.

122
00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:02.769
<v ARSENEAULT>So you had the . . . So there was a garden.

123
00:06:02.769 --> 00:06:04.923
Could you just describe maybe your property?

124
00:06:04.923 --> 00:06:06.462
Like what it looks like?

125
00:06:06.462 --> 00:06:07.077
The yard?

126
00:06:07.077 --> 00:06:07.385
<v ROSAMORE>Then?

127
00:06:07.385 --> 00:06:08.308
Or, which one?

128
00:06:08.308 --> 00:06:12.308
The property that I grew up on, or the property that was mine?

129
00:06:12.308 --> 00:06:16.308
<v ARSENEAULT>Let’s start with the one you grew up on if you can remember.

130
00:06:16.308 --> 00:06:23.385
<v ROSAMORE>Okay well pretty much the property that I grew up on was where I end up living with me and my children later.

131
00:06:23.385 --> 00:06:24.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

132
00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:28.665
<v ROSAMORE>Because my mom and them moved down the street not far from us.

133
00:06:28.665 --> 00:06:34.766
But when I was . . . because we lived in like . . . Still on Evergreen [Road], but in two areas.

134
00:06:34.766 --> 00:06:41.225
Because my mom and them lived in one area and I grew up there for a little while,

135
00:06:41.225 --> 00:06:45.531
and I think that’s when I . . . And then she bought a house.

136
00:06:45.531 --> 00:06:50.914
She moved to where . . . On the corner of Evergreen so then we grew up there.

137
00:06:50.914 --> 00:06:53.067
And that’s where the chickens . . . was.

138
00:06:53.067 --> 00:07:00.962
But then on my dad’s family property is where he kept all the ducks, the geese, the guineas, the emus, all that.

139
00:07:00.962 --> 00:07:01.679
Oh, yeah.

140
00:07:01.679 --> 00:07:07.421
So . . . but the yard was . . . It was like about two big lots where I grew up.

141
00:07:07.421 --> 00:07:10.651
And we had more front yard than back yard,

142
00:07:10.651 --> 00:07:17.828
so it’s kind of like . . . It was just a play yard, but then my mom did all the flower things.

143
00:07:17.828 --> 00:07:18.904
Decorated with flowers.

144
00:07:18.904 --> 00:07:25.364
And my grandma would actually work in the back with her garden every day until she passed on.

145
00:07:25.364 --> 00:07:32.900
And then that’s the last I seen of her garden actually because she was the only one that would do gardens.

146
00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:39.000
So pretty much I don’t remember anything else about a garden as far as that goes.

147
00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:43.774
<v ARSENEAULT>So the grandmother kept a garden, and your father had a livestock at a separate location?

148
00:07:43.774 --> 00:07:50.339
<v ROSAMORE>Well he had the chickens and the biddies where we lived because my brother used to go and gather eggs for him.

149
00:07:50.339 --> 00:07:55.411
And then one time he went to go gather eggs and he thought a snake bit him.

150
00:07:55.411 --> 00:08:00.185
Because they do have chicken snakes, what they called them, and they do love the eggs.

151
00:08:00.185 --> 00:08:04.363
So one day my dad sent my brother to go and check on that.

152
00:08:04.363 --> 00:08:11.226
And it turns out my brother came in the house screaming and hollering because he thought he had gotten bitten, but he didn’t.

153
00:08:11.226 --> 00:08:16.000
But he seen the snake, so I guess he just automatically put it with it.

154
00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:20.425
And where my dad’s mother lived, and some of his sisters lived, and brothers,

155
00:08:20.425 --> 00:08:25.167
that’s where he kept all of the ducks and the geese, the guineas, the emus.

156
00:08:25.167 --> 00:08:30.224
We would get eggs from the emus and there was a big old egg with pretty,

157
00:08:30.224 --> 00:08:33.701
pretty color like a purple . . . Like that color right there, actually.

158
00:08:33.701 --> 00:08:36.230
I mean it was just . . . I don’t know.

159
00:08:36.230 --> 00:08:41.920
It was something to grow up in, and learn, and admire, and be able to teach your children.

160
00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:49.506
It was more like survival for us so that if you ever ever had a point in your life that you could not survive,

161
00:08:49.506 --> 00:08:51.718
he taught us how to actually survive.

162
00:08:51.718 --> 00:08:56.460
Because you could plant a garden and you could grow all the vegetables you need.

163
00:08:56.460 --> 00:09:00.885
And if you got chickens and ducks, well you can eat off of that.

164
00:09:00.885 --> 00:09:03.414
And then he taught us how to fish.

165
00:09:03.414 --> 00:09:10.052
So it’s pretty much if we didn’t have nowhere to . . . No means to get food, we knew how to get food.

166
00:09:10.052 --> 00:09:11.632
You know what I’m saying?

167
00:09:11.632 --> 00:09:14.161
Because it wasn’t expensive to make a garden.

168
00:09:14.161 --> 00:09:17.638
And it wasn’t that expensive to raise chickens, ducks, or whatever.

169
00:09:17.638 --> 00:09:20.799
And fishing wasn’t expensive to just go and catch fish.

170
00:09:20.799 --> 00:09:24.592
And fish were very plentiful back then, as to now, they’re not.

171
00:09:24.592 --> 00:09:34.075
So it’s all . . . I think it was just all about we grew up with family that really want us to be able to know that whenever you get older and

172
00:09:34.075 --> 00:09:39.764
<c.p>you have your own family, that you going to know what’s it’s going to take to make it.

173
00:09:39.764 --> 00:09:43.241
Even if you don’t have money you still can make it.

174
00:09:43.241 --> 00:09:45.770
And that’s what Mossville was all built around.

175
00:09:45.770 --> 00:09:51.144
That’s what our ancestors did because they farmed, and they sowed they crops and stuff like that.

176
00:09:51.144 --> 00:09:57.149
So our parents got it from them and they just brought it on to us and then . . . And now,

177
00:09:57.149 --> 00:09:59.678
because we bring it on to our kids.

178
00:09:59.678 --> 00:10:02.207
Those that really want to get into it.

179
00:10:02.207 --> 00:10:02.523
<v ARSENEAULT>Right.

180
00:10:02.523 --> 00:10:06.000
I think that’s a really good way to grow up.

181
00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:06.373
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

182
00:10:06.373 --> 00:10:07.119
<v ARSENEAULT>Something special.

183
00:10:07.119 --> 00:10:11.220
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah it is because I wouldn’t give it up for nothing.

184
00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:13.458
Because I was happy growing up.

185
00:10:13.458 --> 00:10:18.305
I never had . . . I mean all kids have days where they’re . . . got issues,

186
00:10:18.305 --> 00:10:26.508
but as far as with my parents and the life they gave me, I didn’t have no problems at all with that.

187
00:10:26.508 --> 00:10:32.847
I was more . . . Which I learned and it made me to teach my children what I learned.

188
00:10:32.847 --> 00:10:36.576
And that’s about more than I can say on that.

189
00:10:36.576 --> 00:10:37.695
<v ARSENEAULT>No, that’s great.

190
00:10:37.695 --> 00:10:39.932
Thank you so much for that.

191
00:10:39.932 --> 00:10:46.271
Another question I had was where did you go to school for elementary and for high school?

192
00:10:46.271 --> 00:10:48.136
<v ROSAMORE>Well, I went to Mossville.

193
00:10:48.136 --> 00:10:52.610
It was called Mossville High School at the time when I started.

194
00:10:52.610 --> 00:10:55.220
And I stayed there until . . . In 1975.

195
00:10:55.220 --> 00:10:57.831
And then they came through and decided

196
00:10:57.831 --> 00:11:07.525
<c.p>that they wanted to close Mossville, as far as the school, and they sent half of us starting at Prater Road going towards Sulphur to Sulphur,

197
00:11:07.525 --> 00:11:12.000
and one from Prater Road going to the railroad tracks to Westlake.

198
00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:19.458
So it’s kind of like the ones that we were in school with, after a certain age that we lost.

199
00:11:19.458 --> 00:11:21.322
You know what I’m saying?

200
00:11:21.322 --> 00:11:27.288
It was like they was still living in Mossville, but we all went to different schools.

201
00:11:27.288 --> 00:11:29.898
But we all start off at Mossville.

202
00:11:29.898 --> 00:11:33.627
It was high school then it went to Mossville Elementary.

203
00:11:33.627 --> 00:11:37.729
And I mean I used to be in band in school.

204
00:11:37.729 --> 00:11:42.576
I loved band back in the day and used to play a clarinet.

205
00:11:42.576 --> 00:11:45.186
And it was like . . . I don’t know.

206
00:11:45.186 --> 00:11:50.407
It was our little . . . Our own little community school and everybody was happy there.

207
00:11:50.407 --> 00:11:53.763
And we all had to be moved around, so.

208
00:11:53.763 --> 00:11:56.000
But that’s life I guess.

209
00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:58.366
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember some of your teachers?

210
00:11:58.366 --> 00:11:58.704
<v ROSAMORE>Yes.

211
00:11:58.704 --> 00:12:01.746
There was one . . . Well, I remember one in particular.

212
00:12:01.746 --> 00:12:05.801
She was my first grade teacher and her name was Ms. Beverly.

213
00:12:05.801 --> 00:12:08.167
And she was a real short lady.

214
00:12:08.167 --> 00:12:09.857
She was real real sweet.

215
00:12:09.857 --> 00:12:14.927
And then one day when I came to school they said she had passed away.

216
00:12:14.927 --> 00:12:20.334
And that’s like a memory that I’ll never forget because she was a sweet sweet lady.

217
00:12:20.334 --> 00:12:27.432
And then you come . . . Well, you know they’re there one day, and that was hard for a young child to concept.

218
00:12:27.432 --> 00:12:29.122
You know what I’m saying?

219
00:12:29.122 --> 00:12:36.895
Because one day she was there and then we found out . . . we would come back the next week or so, she passed away.

220
00:12:36.895 --> 00:12:38.585
And I’ve always remembered that.

221
00:12:38.585 --> 00:12:40.951
And then I had this other teacher.

222
00:12:40.951 --> 00:12:42.303
It was Ms. Madison.

223
00:12:42.303 --> 00:12:43.993
She was our P.E. teacher.

224
00:12:43.993 --> 00:12:45.007
Very strong woman.

225
00:12:45.007 --> 00:12:46.697
She taught us a lot.

226
00:12:46.697 --> 00:12:48.725
I mean she was real good.

227
00:12:48.725 --> 00:12:52.105
Then we had Mr. Mouton, which was our math teacher.

228
00:12:52.105 --> 00:12:55.146
Then we had Mr. Dyez, which was our history.

229
00:12:55.146 --> 00:13:00.216
Then we had Ms. Reynolds which was our . . . I want to say she was science.

230
00:13:00.216 --> 00:13:01.906
Then we had Ms. Sacherie.

231
00:13:01.906 --> 00:13:02.244
Now

232
00:13:02.244 --> 00:13:10.017
<c.p>she’s Karen Perry and thank God that she’s still living because she came to our town Mossville thing we had here last year.

233
00:13:10.017 --> 00:13:14.749
And let me see, I’m trying to think of anybody else that I remember.

234
00:13:14.749 --> 00:13:20.157
Because certain ones you carry on . . . You carry with you for the rest of your life.

235
00:13:20.157 --> 00:13:24.213
It’s just certain ways that they carry themselves that you always remember.

236
00:13:24.213 --> 00:13:26.578
And those were pretty much the ones.

237
00:13:26.578 --> 00:13:33.000
I mean I’m sure I had more than that, but that’s the ones that I remember pretty much.

238
00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:33.313
<v ARSENEAULT>Awesome.

239
00:13:33.313 --> 00:13:35.507
Did you decide to go to college?

240
00:13:35.507 --> 00:13:38.327
I saw from your sheet that you’re an LPN.

241
00:13:38.327 --> 00:13:38.641
<v ROSAMORE>Yes.

242
00:13:38.641 --> 00:13:44.282
Well I went through numerous schools because I was trying to decide what I really wanted to be.

243
00:13:44.282 --> 00:13:44.595
<v ARSENEAULT>[Agrees].

244
00:13:44.595 --> 00:13:47.729
<v ROSAMORE>And I wasn’t sure so I went through CNA school.

245
00:13:47.729 --> 00:13:48.982
I graduated from that.

246
00:13:48.982 --> 00:13:50.863
I worked for that a while.

247
00:13:50.863 --> 00:13:52.116
Then I’m like, “No,”

248
00:13:52.116 --> 00:13:58.384
because by that time I had children and they weren’t paying what . . . Really enough for all the kids I had.

249
00:13:58.384 --> 00:14:02.144
So then I end up going to McNeese to be a teacher.

250
00:14:02.144 --> 00:14:06.532
Well, I got into that a little while then I said, “No, no, no,”

251
00:14:06.532 --> 00:14:11.546
because my calling, I think, is taking care of the sick, and the young, or old.

252
00:14:11.546 --> 00:14:17.187
So I said, “Well,” I said, “Well Momma,” I said, “I think I’m going to just try nursing.

253
00:14:17.187 --> 00:14:20.634
I don’t know how good I’m going to be with it.”

254
00:14:20.634 --> 00:14:22.514
Because I did CNA for years.

255
00:14:22.514 --> 00:14:27.215
Then I say, “Well, my children are getting older,” and not because of the money.

256
00:14:27.215 --> 00:14:32.856
I think my heart was already there with it because I’ve been doing that now for eighteen years.

257
00:14:32.856 --> 00:14:33.796
And so I

258
00:14:33.796 --> 00:14:36.930
<c.p>went to school for that and it was a struggle,

259
00:14:36.930 --> 00:14:44.137
but helps with my mom and my dad keeping my children so I could study and go and meet other girls to study.

260
00:14:44.137 --> 00:14:49.778
And I cried a many days because I thought I wasn’t going to make it, but I did.

261
00:14:49.778 --> 00:14:52.599
So God stepped in and said, “Oh, no baby.

262
00:14:52.599 --> 00:14:54.165
You going to make it.”

263
00:14:54.165 --> 00:15:02.000
Because I had a strong willed mind and I had children I wanted . . . I wanted to make sure I took care of my children.

264
00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:05.977
I was married at one time, but that went to . . . Out the window.

265
00:15:05.977 --> 00:15:13.320
So then I had to stand up and be a woman about things and just take it up on my own and I did.

266
00:15:13.320 --> 00:15:18.215
And so I went to Sowela [Technical Community College] and I graduated in nursing for that.

267
00:15:18.215 --> 00:15:20.968
And I’ve been doing nursing now for eighteen years.

268
00:15:20.968 --> 00:15:26.781
And I’ve really basically worked a lot in a nursing home taking care of the old and the sick.

269
00:15:26.781 --> 00:15:31.982
And now I’m working taking care of . . . Well, I’m working at the sheriff department now pretty much.

270
00:15:31.982 --> 00:15:34.429
And I like what I do there, too,

271
00:15:34.429 --> 00:15:38.712
as well so . . . And I did go to McNeese again later, but then . . . No,

272
00:15:38.712 --> 00:15:44.219
I went to Lamar for a whole year for the RN program and I had a 3.9 average,

273
00:15:44.219 --> 00:15:48.808
but when Hurricane Rita came I just felt like I didn’t want to do that.

274
00:15:48.808 --> 00:15:50.338
You know what I’m saying?

275
00:15:50.338 --> 00:15:52.479
Because I liked what I did already,

276
00:15:52.479 --> 00:15:58.598
so I didn’t want to have too much responsibilities on me to where I could not focus on my kids,

277
00:15:58.598 --> 00:16:02.269
or anything else because I’m so worried about all this other stuff.

278
00:16:02.269 --> 00:16:08.082
And so I just let that go, but as of right now I’ve been a LPN for eighteen years.

279
00:16:08.082 --> 00:16:09.000
Almost nineteen.

280
00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:09.268
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

281
00:16:09.268 --> 00:16:09.805
That’s awesome.

282
00:16:09.805 --> 00:16:10.074
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

283
00:16:10.074 --> 00:16:12.221
<v ARSENEAULT>It does take a special person, I think . . .

284
00:16:12.221 --> 00:16:12.758
<v ROSAMORE>Oh, yeah.

285
00:16:12.758 --> 00:16:13.295
Oh, yeah.

286
00:16:13.295 --> 00:16:18.664
I mean you got to have it from the heart, or you might as well not even waste your time,

287
00:16:18.664 --> 00:16:22.960
or waste nobody else time because if you can’t love them like you love your own,

288
00:16:22.960 --> 00:16:28.060
your own mom, or your own grandma, your own grandpa, then how can you really take care of them?

289
00:16:28.060 --> 00:16:28.866
Because you can’t.

290
00:16:28.866 --> 00:16:33.698
I mean you got to have the heart in your heart for it, not just say, “I can.”

291
00:16:33.698 --> 00:16:36.114
You got to really have it in your heart.

292
00:16:36.114 --> 00:16:42.826
And I knew I had it when I went through all these other schools and I ended up back where I was at square one.

293
00:16:42.826 --> 00:16:49.000
So I knew that’s where God wanted me to be really, and that’s what I did, and I’m still doing it now.

294
00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:55.429
<v ARSENEAULT>Which places were the most important, or still are the most important places in Mossville?

295
00:16:55.429 --> 00:16:57.143
Like the buildings, locations . . .

296
00:16:57.143 --> 00:17:04.429
<v ROSAMORE>We used to have this building right next to the gym that was called the Masonic Hall.

297
00:17:04.429 --> 00:17:05.286
And that’s . . .

298
00:17:05.286 --> 00:17:07.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Can you spell that?

299
00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:07.429
Or . . .

300
00:17:07.429 --> 00:17:09.143
<v ROSAMORE>The Masonic Hall . . . Yeah.

301
00:17:09.143 --> 00:17:10.000
M-A-S-O-N-I-C.

302
00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:10.500
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, oh.

303
00:17:10.500 --> 00:17:11.250
Yeah yeah yeah.

304
00:17:11.250 --> 00:17:11.500
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

305
00:17:11.500 --> 00:17:12.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

306
00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:15.000
<v ROSAMORE>And that’s where all of the dancers were going.

307
00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:15.667
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, okay.

308
00:17:15.667 --> 00:17:17.333
<v ROSAMORE>Like for the school children.

309
00:17:17.333 --> 00:17:21.667
We would have like what they call record hops and stuff like that.

310
00:17:21.667 --> 00:17:26.667
And then there was a lady that lived here, and I think she’s still here.

311
00:17:26.667 --> 00:17:30.000
Well she used to have what they call a canteen.

312
00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:31.333
Mrs. Risa Mae Mouton.

313
00:17:31.333 --> 00:17:38.333
She used to have what they call a canteen for all the young kids to go . . . keep us out of trouble.

314
00:17:38.333 --> 00:17:43.333
And I thought that was nice, but then that closed off for a little while.

315
00:17:43.333 --> 00:17:48.000
And then the Recreation and the pool was a lot for us to do.

316
00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:49.667
You know what I’m saying?

317
00:17:49.667 --> 00:17:51.667
It kept us out of trouble.

318
00:17:51.667 --> 00:17:55.333
But then back in our days, children found things to do.

319
00:17:55.333 --> 00:18:00.667
I mean we . . . Well, me personally, myself because we did a lot of fishing and camping,

320
00:18:00.667 --> 00:18:08.667
but my . . . But I think my dad and mom did that just to keep us from getting out of hand and getting in trouble.

321
00:18:08.667 --> 00:18:14.333
And then we used to have a lot of pool parties here for like birthdays or whatever.

322
00:18:14.333 --> 00:18:15.667
<v ARSENEAULT>Was the pool here . . .?

323
00:18:15.667 --> 00:18:17.000
<v ROSAMORE>Yes, the pool . . .

324
00:18:17.000 --> 00:18:18.200
<v ARSENEAULT>At the Rigmaiden Center?

325
00:18:18.200 --> 00:18:21.200
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah the pool was right where it is right now.

326
00:18:21.200 --> 00:18:21.800
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, okay.

327
00:18:21.800 --> 00:18:23.300
<v ROSAMORE>But it was made different.

328
00:18:23.300 --> 00:18:27.800
It was a little bit different made, but that’s where all the pool parties was.

329
00:18:27.800 --> 00:18:35.000
And then we had like a recreation center at one point, but it wasn’t made like this, but we always had a recreation center.

330
00:18:35.000 --> 00:18:35.300
Always.

331
00:18:35.300 --> 00:18:39.200
And that’s where everybody would come and shoot basketball and stuff like that.

332
00:18:39.200 --> 00:18:50.300
And then we had a little store right down the street over there it was called Bell 7-11 where they would actually put video games for us to go in the store and actually you could play.

333
00:18:50.300 --> 00:18:53.300
And that kept a lot of us out of trouble.

334
00:18:53.300 --> 00:18:55.400
A lot of us out of trouble.

335
00:18:55.400 --> 00:19:00.800
Because it could have been really . . . I mean back in them days, it could have been really bad,

336
00:19:00.800 --> 00:19:05.300
but our parents always seemed to be . . . This things constructive in staying out of trouble.

337
00:19:05.300 --> 00:19:10.100
So if that mean carrying you on the river all weekend, then that’s what they did.

338
00:19:10.100 --> 00:19:14.900
And so for me doing that all my life growing up, well, I’m still doing that.

339
00:19:14.900 --> 00:19:23.600
I have a fishing pond at the place where I live so that my grandkids . . . And I take them out there so I can teach them how to fish.

340
00:19:23.600 --> 00:19:25.700
It’s . . . I mean it’s all about values.

341
00:19:25.700 --> 00:19:26.900
What you’ve been taught.

342
00:19:26.900 --> 00:19:31.400
And you can carry that on if you choose to, or you don’t have to.

343
00:19:31.400 --> 00:19:33.200
It just depends on the individual.

344
00:19:33.200 --> 00:19:35.600
But if you’re taught well, you’re going to.

345
00:19:35.600 --> 00:19:41.600
You’re going to carry it on to your children, and your children are going to carry it on to theirs.

346
00:19:41.600 --> 00:19:44.000
That’s the way I see it anyway.

347
00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:47.518
<v ARSENEAULT>It’s a good legacy to leave for your family.

348
00:19:47.518 --> 00:19:47.909
<v ROSAMORE>Yes.

349
00:19:47.909 --> 00:19:48.300
Yes.

350
00:19:48.300 --> 00:19:56.118
<v ARSENEAULT>What is . . . this is kind of a . . . You can take a second to think about it if you need to,

351
00:19:56.118 --> 00:19:58.855
but what does home mean to you?

352
00:19:58.855 --> 00:20:01.200
<v ROSAMORE>Home is where I grew up.

353
00:20:01.200 --> 00:20:05.500
All my memories of my grandmother, my great grandfather, my uncles,

354
00:20:05.500 --> 00:20:11.755
and what my children . . . I grew them up here so I still have memories from that.

355
00:20:11.755 --> 00:20:17.618
Of how they grew up and what I went through to see them grow up.

356
00:20:17.618 --> 00:20:20.745
I mean home is where the heart is.

357
00:20:20.745 --> 00:20:24.264
I mean there’s no other way to put it.

358
00:20:24.264 --> 00:20:27.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How many children do you have?

359
00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:28.250
<v ROSAMORE>I have four.

360
00:20:28.250 --> 00:20:29.500
And fourteen grandchildren.

361
00:20:29.500 --> 00:20:29.917
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow!

362
00:20:29.917 --> 00:20:30.333
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

363
00:20:30.333 --> 00:20:32.000
Ain’t that something, girl?

364
00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:32.417
Yeah.

365
00:20:32.417 --> 00:20:35.333
I got four kids and fourteen grandchildren.

366
00:20:35.333 --> 00:20:35.750
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

367
00:20:35.750 --> 00:20:37.000
That’s awesome.

368
00:20:37.000 --> 00:20:37.265
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

369
00:20:37.265 --> 00:20:38.325
And I’m like, “God!”

370
00:20:38.325 --> 00:20:42.564
But God only give you more than what . . . No more than what you can handle anyway.

371
00:20:42.564 --> 00:20:49.188
And I’m happy with every last one of them, but I just never thought I was going to have fourteen grandchildren out of four children.

372
00:20:49.188 --> 00:20:55.017
<c.p>But then one of my daughters [?], she has a lot, but she have . . . Well I got two daughters that has identical twins.

373
00:20:55.017 --> 00:20:55.547
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, wow.

374
00:20:55.547 --> 00:20:57.402
<v ROSAMORE>Because my mom is an identical twin.

375
00:20:57.402 --> 00:20:58.726
<v ARSENEAULT>And it skips a generation.

376
00:20:58.726 --> 00:20:58.991
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

377
00:20:58.991 --> 00:21:01.376
And I thank God it didn’t hit me though.

378
00:21:01.376 --> 00:21:01.641
<v ARSENEAULT>Right.

379
00:21:01.641 --> 00:21:02.436
<v ROSAMORE>You know, really?

380
00:21:02.436 --> 00:21:03.761
But it hit my daughters.

381
00:21:03.761 --> 00:21:06.675
So that’s why I got so many because twins and that.

382
00:21:06.675 --> 00:21:08.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Twins in the family.

383
00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:08.299
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

384
00:21:08.299 --> 00:21:11.284
But really and truly home is where the heart is.

385
00:21:11.284 --> 00:21:13.970
I mean there’s no other way to tell it.

386
00:21:13.970 --> 00:21:21.134
You got to love where you are, and you got to love the lifestyle you have to be happy where you going to be.

387
00:21:21.134 --> 00:21:22.925
And it is what it is.

388
00:21:22.925 --> 00:21:25.015
And home is where the heart is.

389
00:21:25.015 --> 00:21:27.104
<v ARSENEAULT>So what is community mean to you?

390
00:21:27.104 --> 00:21:28.000
What does . . .?

391
00:21:28.000 --> 00:21:30.871
<v ROSAMORE>Well Mossville as far as the community to me,

392
00:21:30.871 --> 00:21:38.207
it was a very important part of my life too because everybody here was my family, and I grew up around my family.

393
00:21:38.207 --> 00:21:44.267
And when you grow up around your family, it’s a big difference than growing up in a strange place.

394
00:21:44.267 --> 00:21:47.776
You don’t know no one, which is where I’m at now.

395
00:21:47.776 --> 00:21:49.371
You know what I’m saying?

396
00:21:49.371 --> 00:21:51.284
And I’ve been trying to adjust.

397
00:21:51.284 --> 00:21:54.793
I’ve been there for almost a year, but I miss home.

398
00:21:54.793 --> 00:21:59.259
I really do because I got all my relatives . . . Some of them still here.

399
00:21:59.259 --> 00:22:05.000
And my kids, they’re with me, but when it comes to a community, well you got family.

400
00:22:05.000 --> 00:22:12.000
I mean even if you leave your home, say unlocked, and you say you’re going to go to Walmart,

401
00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:17.158
well you know that your neighbor going to watch your house, and vice versa.

402
00:22:17.158 --> 00:22:22.684
If they leave they going to tell you, “Hey I’m going to the doctor’s office.”

403
00:22:22.684 --> 00:22:29.316
Well that’s letting me know, okay well let me keep my eyes on his place, or her place.

404
00:22:29.316 --> 00:22:32.263
So it’s pretty much . . . Mossville is my family.

405
00:22:32.263 --> 00:22:39.632
So it’s like the community was my family because almost everybody here is related to me except a few families.

406
00:22:39.632 --> 00:22:42.947
So I mean it’s all about family there, too.

407
00:22:42.947 --> 00:22:48.105
It’s what you . . . It’s who you around and what your environment is consist of.

408
00:22:48.105 --> 00:22:51.421
And to me that was my family too, so.

409
00:22:51.421 --> 00:22:55.105
And when I had to move, I’m leaving my family.

410
00:22:55.105 --> 00:22:55.842
Pretty much.

411
00:22:55.842 --> 00:23:05.421
I mean all of the older ones are gone like my dad and his . . . He still have sisters and brothers left here that still lives here.

412
00:23:05.421 --> 00:23:14.632
So I still comes and visit them, but when I come here now and see well, from where I grew up, it’s not the same.

413
00:23:14.632 --> 00:23:16.474
It’s nowhere near the same.

414
00:23:16.474 --> 00:23:24.211
So in a way I’m kind of glad I’m gone because the memories going to always be in your heart anyway.

415
00:23:24.211 --> 00:23:33.789
You might have a reservoir that you keep it in and bring it out every now and then, but you’ll never forget where you come from.

416
00:23:33.789 --> 00:23:36.368
Whether it’s . . . I mean you always remember,

417
00:23:36.368 --> 00:23:42.263
hey . . . because my cousins lived around for Christmases I mean we had wonderful times with everybody.

418
00:23:42.263 --> 00:23:46.684
It was just something you’ll never forget, no matter where you move.

419
00:23:46.684 --> 00:23:49.263
I mean you can always make new

420
00:23:49.263 --> 00:23:53.316
<c.p>memories, but your old memories going to always be there, too.

421
00:23:53.316 --> 00:23:55.895
So you just got to move on.

422
00:23:55.895 --> 00:23:57.000
Pretty much.

423
00:23:57.000 --> 00:23:58.062
<v ARSENEAULT>That makes sense.

424
00:23:58.062 --> 00:23:58.416
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

425
00:23:58.416 --> 00:24:01.247
I mean that’s all it is to it.

426
00:24:01.247 --> 00:24:08.680
Everything is about memory and what you let your heart get . . . go in your heart, and what you let come out.

427
00:24:08.680 --> 00:24:12.928
If you don’t want to have no memories, it’s going to leave.

428
00:24:12.928 --> 00:24:18.945
If you want to keep those memories for the rest of your life, it’s going to stay.

429
00:24:18.945 --> 00:24:21.423
It’s just what you want to do.

430
00:24:21.423 --> 00:24:26.024
And I’m willing to let it stay because I’ve been there fifty-three years.

431
00:24:26.024 --> 00:24:27.794
I can’t let it go.

432
00:24:27.794 --> 00:24:29.210
It’s just not good.

433
00:24:29.210 --> 00:24:30.625
Not for me anyway.

434
00:24:30.625 --> 00:24:36.997
<v ARSENEAULT>What’s the most important thing you want people to remember about Mossville that isn’t in the history books?

435
00:24:36.997 --> 00:24:43.014
<v ROSAMORE>That it was a place where my ancestors, free slaves, came and made a place for us.

436
00:24:43.014 --> 00:24:47.615
Might have took their life away later on, you know what I’m saying?

437
00:24:47.615 --> 00:24:50.801
But it was a place they made for us.

438
00:24:50.801 --> 00:24:54.694
And for a long time nobody could take it from us.

439
00:24:54.694 --> 00:24:57.172
I mean it is what it is.

440
00:24:57.172 --> 00:25:03.189
I want it to always be known that Mossville was here, and there was loving people here.

441
00:25:03.189 --> 00:25:08.498
No matter how you look at it we . . . because it’s . . . It was a loving community.

442
00:25:08.498 --> 00:25:10.268
You can’t find that everywhere.

443
00:25:10.268 --> 00:25:16.993
I mean I remember growing up when . . . I don’t care if my daddy saw you fifteen times that day.

444
00:25:16.993 --> 00:25:25.488
He was going to wave to you fifteen times that day, and that’s how I grew up and I got my children doing that.

445
00:25:25.488 --> 00:25:29.735
Don’t ever look a person in the face and do not speak.

446
00:25:29.735 --> 00:25:30.443
That’s the

447
00:25:30.443 --> 00:25:31.859
<c.p>way I was raised.

448
00:25:31.859 --> 00:25:40.000
And it’s all about how you was raised so I don’t want that to ever be forgotten because we were happy people.

449
00:25:40.000 --> 00:25:46.255
Our families taught us all the morals and what we needed to know to carry on and live somewhere else.

450
00:25:46.255 --> 00:25:50.320
And now that I’m somewhere else, I still carries my morals with me.

451
00:25:50.320 --> 00:25:54.073
And I make sure my children and my grandkids carry the morals.

452
00:25:54.073 --> 00:25:58.451
I mean it’s all about . . . I mean it’s going to always be our home.

453
00:25:58.451 --> 00:26:01.578
No matter if the plant take over the whole nation,

454
00:26:01.578 --> 00:26:07.207
we’re going to always know that Mossville was here and I don’t want it to ever be gone.

455
00:26:07.207 --> 00:26:10.022
Not really in our minds, and in history books.

456
00:26:10.022 --> 00:26:14.713
Because you got all these other little places where you see in the history books.

457
00:26:14.713 --> 00:26:21.280
Well why can’t Mossville be in there because I mean it’s like we . . . At my age I wasn’t ready to move,

458
00:26:21.280 --> 00:26:26.284
but I’d rather move with my family than to deal with what all was coming around,

459
00:26:26.284 --> 00:26:34.727
but then I don’t want to be a person that makes it hard for them because they got to do what they have to do as well.

460
00:26:34.727 --> 00:26:37.542
So . . . but it ain’t like they didn’t help us.

461
00:26:37.542 --> 00:26:38.480
They really did.

462
00:26:38.480 --> 00:26:46.298
So it made us where we could go somewhere else and have everything paid for if we choose to and just start a new life,

463
00:26:46.298 --> 00:26:48.800
but we’re still going to have our memories.

464
00:26:48.800 --> 00:26:56.931
Because I was told that they were also helping with this part of this and I think that’s great, because they didn’t have to do that.

465
00:26:56.931 --> 00:27:00.996
They could have just bought us all and say, “To hell with it.”

466
00:27:00.996 --> 00:27:02.560
You know what I’m saying?

467
00:27:02.560 --> 00:27:06.000
Y’all can cut that out if y’all want to [laughs].

468
00:27:06.000 --> 00:27:06.319
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

469
00:27:06.319 --> 00:27:08.553
<v ROSAMORE>But I’m saying they didn’t have to.

470
00:27:08.553 --> 00:27:13.021
But they thinking of us in so many ways that people don’t even see.

471
00:27:13.021 --> 00:27:17.489
Because they could have left it where we was just off the map completely.

472
00:27:17.489 --> 00:27:21.319
And now we . . . If God’s willing, we won’t be off the map.

473
00:27:21.319 --> 00:27:22.915
We will be brought to

474
00:27:22.915 --> 00:27:28.979
<c.p>life again in the libraries and the museums so that anybody that want to know what Mossville was like,

475
00:27:28.979 --> 00:27:34.085
they can go and read up on it, or they can go look at the videos.

476
00:27:34.085 --> 00:27:36.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Or listen to this interview.

477
00:27:36.000 --> 00:27:43.029
<v ROSAMORE>Or listen to my interview [laughs] because I am one that talks, honey, but I love to talk what I believe in and truth.

478
00:27:43.029 --> 00:27:45.079
And that’s just the way I feel.

479
00:27:45.079 --> 00:27:49.764
I mean as long as Mossville is not forgotten because my ancestors came here and said,

480
00:27:49.764 --> 00:27:52.986
“Hey this is where I want my family to grow up.”

481
00:27:52.986 --> 00:27:58.257
And by them moving there, having children, them children having children, that’s how we got where we were.

482
00:27:58.257 --> 00:28:04.993
Well, per se, my dad and his siblings and his dad, but it’s all family anyway either way you look at it, so.

483
00:28:04.993 --> 00:28:12.314
Yeah I don’t want it to ever be forgotten, no. Because I think it was a little small town, but it was still our home.

484
00:28:12.314 --> 00:28:14.071
It was . . . Everybody was loving families.

485
00:28:14.071 --> 00:28:23.150
I mean we all helped each other out or if our . . . See like if one of our children was getting into some trouble and if it’s a neighbor that saw it,

486
00:28:23.150 --> 00:28:24.614
they could whoop your children.

487
00:28:24.614 --> 00:28:29.886
And then when you . . . When they come home, you going to get a whooping from Momma and Daddy.

488
00:28:29.886 --> 00:28:36.621
And that’s what I loved because everybody watched us even though we might of was not doing the right thing all the time,

489
00:28:36.621 --> 00:28:42.479
but at least we knew that we going to think twice because we know that so and so watching us,

490
00:28:42.479 --> 00:28:44.821
she’ll go back and tell Mom and Dad.

491
00:28:44.821 --> 00:28:46.579
See, and that’s how it was.

492
00:28:46.579 --> 00:28:48.629
So you can’t find that too much.

493
00:28:48.629 --> 00:28:53.021
You probably can in other places, but I’ve never lived there so I don’t know.

494
00:28:53.021 --> 00:28:55.071
But I know what happened in Mossville.

495
00:28:55.071 --> 00:28:55.657
I mean . . .

496
00:28:55.657 --> 00:28:58.000
<v ARSENEAULT>The community took care of each other?

497
00:28:58.000 --> 00:28:58.385
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

498
00:28:58.385 --> 00:28:59.154
Pretty much.

499
00:28:59.154 --> 00:29:04.923
We just all family though like I said again, we all was some related somehow.

500
00:29:04.923 --> 00:29:11.846
Because everybody that was on that little list of the people that made Mossville, that’s all my family.

501
00:29:11.846 --> 00:29:13.000
All my family.

502
00:29:13.000 --> 00:29:15.308
Someway or another we all related.

503
00:29:15.308 --> 00:29:15.692
<v ARSENEAULT>Somehow . . .

504
00:29:15.692 --> 00:29:16.077
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

505
00:29:16.077 --> 00:29:18.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Some kind of way.

506
00:29:18.000 --> 00:29:18.423
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

507
00:29:18.423 --> 00:29:19.269
We are.

508
00:29:19.269 --> 00:29:19.692
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

509
00:29:19.692 --> 00:29:23.077
Well I think our time is almost up.

510
00:29:23.077 --> 00:29:29.000
On behalf of the center and on the museum, we appreciate your contribution today.

511
00:29:29.000 --> 00:29:29.423
<v ROSAMORE>Okay.

512
00:29:29.423 --> 00:29:34.077
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you have anything that you want to add in closing?

513
00:29:34.077 --> 00:29:43.385
<v ROSAMORE>Well I think this is a very very good thing that’s happening right now because they give everyone that want a voice,

514
00:29:43.385 --> 00:29:49.731
how they feel about where they lived, this is the best way to do it.

515
00:29:49.731 --> 00:29:56.500
I mean because you can express and just say what you feel, but the right things.

516
00:29:56.500 --> 00:29:59.462
You can X that off, too [laughs].

517
00:29:59.462 --> 00:30:02.000
But at least we can . . .

518
00:30:02.000 --> 00:30:07.411
<v ARSENEAULT>No, we do want to get your . . . we want to get everyone’s stories however they want to tell them.

519
00:30:07.411 --> 00:30:07.696
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

520
00:30:07.696 --> 00:30:11.399
And I don’t want to move and then it’s forgotten to me either.

521
00:30:11.399 --> 00:30:16.241
And this I feel no matter what y’all put in there, I’m going to be there somewhere.

522
00:30:16.241 --> 00:30:17.665
You know what I’m saying?

523
00:30:17.665 --> 00:30:24.500
And I’m going to feel good to know, hey, I was there to be able to say what my little town was all about.

524
00:30:24.500 --> 00:30:32.759
And that’s going to carry me on until the day I die because I was able to sit down with people I don’t know and they listened to me,

525
00:30:32.759 --> 00:30:37.886
and they listened to what I had to say about my town, and my ancestors, and everybody else.

526
00:30:37.886 --> 00:30:39.880
So I’m glad y’all did this, actually.

527
00:30:39.880 --> 00:30:45.291
I was nervous at first, but I come out of that though when y’all made me feel so comfortable.

528
00:30:45.291 --> 00:30:47.000
<v WILLCOX>Aw I’m glad we did.

529
00:30:47.000 --> 00:30:47.938
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah I was very nervous.

530
00:30:47.938 --> 00:30:51.312
<v ARSENEAULT>Thank you so so much and we’ll be in touch with the possibility of a follow up interview.

531
00:30:51.312 --> 00:30:51.512
<v ROSAMORE>Okay.

532
00:30:51.500 --> 00:30:53.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And just thank you so much for . . .

533
00:30:53.000 --> 00:30:53.333
<v ROSAMORE>Alright.

534
00:30:53.333 --> 00:30:53.667
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

535
00:30:53.667 --> 00:30:54.333
<v ROSAMORE>Sounds good.

536
00:30:54.333 --> 00:30:55.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

537
00:30:55.000 --> 00:30:55.429
<c.p>[End Tape 4406.

538
00:30:55.429 --> 00:30:55.857
End Session I.]
